  "articleBody": "It's a common puzzle: you've added schema markup to your site, expecting those fancy rich results in Google, but they're just not showing up. It can feel like you've done all the right things, but the search engine isn't playing along. The truth is, while valid structured data makes your page eligible for rich results, it doesn't guarantee them. Google has its own criteria and doesn't always show them, even if your markup is technically perfect.\n\nOne key reason could be that the information you've marked up isn't actually visible on the page to users. Google is pretty clear that schema should reflect what's visually present. Another frequent issue is using an unsuitable schema type for your content, or perhaps omitting a required property that Google needs to understand the context. Conflicting or inaccurate values within your markup can also trip things up. Sometimes, it's simpler: your pages might be blocked by `robots.txt` or have a `noindex` tag, which prevents Google from fully processing the content, including your schema.\n\nWhat to Prepare Before You Start: Before diving into fixing schema, first clearly identify the specific content on your page that you want to highlight. Know whether it's a `Service`, `Article`, `LocalBusiness`, or `FAQPage` data you're trying to describe. Gather all the required property values for that schema type (like a business address for `LocalBusiness` or question/answer pairs for `FAQPage`). Having a clear understanding of your on-page content and what schema type best represents it will make the troubleshooting process much smoother. Also, have a list of the URLs where you've deployed the markup, as this will be essential for testing.\n\nAnother thing to consider is the actual content quality. Even if schema is technically correct, Google prioritizes high-quality, relevant content. If your page isn't providing a good user experience or lacks substantial information, rich results might not appear. We always recommend starting with solid, factual content first, then layering schema on top to help search engines understand it better. It’s not just about syntax; it’s about providing valuable answers that AI systems want to cite.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-23"
